STM32L432 Advanced Motor Speed Controller with Overspeed Alarm

Author : Martins Osemudiamen Okoh.
Embedded Systems Project – EENG1030 Course

Project Description
This embedded system project implements a real-time motor speed monitoring and control system using an STM32L432 microcontroller. The system reads rotational speed from a quadrature encoder, controls motor speed via PWM output, provides visual feedback using an LED, and activates an audible buzzer alarm when the motor exceeds a safe speed threshold of 3500 RPM. A serial debug interface and SPI display provide real-time status information.

The video showcase of this project can be viewed via: https://youtu.be/aQIbt7y0xQ4?si=WVbYLmp21I6872UJ

Hardware Requirements
STM32L432 microcontroller board (e.g., Nucleo-L432KC)
Quadrature rotary encoder (2 pulses per revolution)
DC FAN
LED (visual status indicator)
330 Ohms resistor
Buzzer (audible alarm)
LCD display
USB-to-UART converter for serial debugging

Pin Connections
Component	Pin	Configuration
Encoder CLK	PA0	TIM2 CH1 (AF1)
Encoder DT	PB3	TIM2 CH2 (AF1)
PWM Output	PA6	TIM16 CH1 (AF14)
Tachometer Input	PB4	Digital input with pull-up
Status LED	PB5	Digital output
Buzzer	PB6	Digital output
UART TX	Assigned	Serial output at 9600 baud

Features
Encoder reading – Timer 2 configured in quadrature encoder mode (mode 3) counts pulses from both encoder channels
PWM generation – Timer 16 produces a 25 kHz PWM signal with adjustable duty cycle based on encoder position
Speed calculation – Converts encoder pulses to revolutions per minute using a measurement interval of 0.1333 seconds
Overspeed detection – Triggers alarm when RPM exceeds 3500
Visual alarm – LED turns off during overspeed condition (normally on when speed is safe)
Audible alarm – Buzzer generates a 500 Hz square wave using Timer 6 interrupts
Serial monitoring – Outputs encoder value, PWM duty, and RPM to serial console
Display support – SPI display shows encoder position and current RPM

Key Parameters
Parameter	Value
System clock	80 MHz
Pulses per revolution	2
PWM frequency	25 kHz
PWM resolution	3200 steps (0–3199)
RPM safe threshold	3500 RPM
Buzzer frequency	500 Hz
Buzzer timer interrupt rate	1000 Hz (1 ms)
Main loop update rate	10 Hz (100 ms delay)


Register Configuration Summary

Timer 2 (Encoder)
Mode: Quadrature encoder mode 3 (counts on both edges)
ARR: 159 (full scale count range)
Inputs: PA0 (TI1) and PB3 (TI2) as capture inputs

Timer 6 (Buzzer)
Prescaler: 7999 (divides 80 MHz to 10 kHz)
ARR: 9 (produces 1 kHz update events)
Interrupt: Toggles PB6 at 500 Hz when playing = 1

Timer 16 (PWM)
Prescaler: 0
ARR: 3199 (25 kHz PWM)
CCR1: 1600 (50% duty cycle, adjustable)
BDTR: MOE bit set (required for output)

Software Structure
                   
Main Loop Operation
Read encoder counter (TIM2->CNT) as motor position/voltage
Scale value and update PWM duty cycle (TIM16->CCR1)
Safely copy interrupt-driven pulse_count (interrupts disabled briefly)
Calculate speed in Hz and RPM
Check overspeed condition and control LED/buzzer
Output status via serial
Update SPI display with position and RPM
Delay 100 ms and repeat

Interrupt Handlers
                   
TIM6_DAC_IRQHandler – Toggles buzzer pin at 500 Hz when playing = 1, ensures buzzer is off when playing = 0
EXTI4_IRQHandler – Counts tachometer pulses on PB4 (incrementing pulse_count)

Speed Calculation Formula
                
speed_hz = pulse_count / pulse_per_rev
speed_rpm = speed_hz × 450
The factor 450 assumes a 0.1333 second measurement interval (60 seconds ÷ 0.1333 = 450).

Serial Output
Initialization verification:

MODER PA6: 2
AFR PA6: 14
TIM16 CR1: 129
TIM16 CCER: 1
TIM16 BDTR: 32768
TIM16 ARR: 3199
TIM16 CCR1: 1600
Runtime monitoring:

ENC: 45, CCR1: 905, RPM: 1250, buzz: 0
ENC: 78, CCR1: 1568, RPM: 2150, buzz: 0
ENC: 120, CCR1: 2415, RPM: 3300, buzz: 0
ENC: 155, CCR1: 3118, RPM: 4250, buzz: 1   ← Overspeed!
Building and Flashing
                   
Prerequisites
ARM GCC toolchain (arm-none-eabi-gcc)
STM32CubeProgrammer or OpenOCD
ST-Link debugger (built into Nucleo boards)


Troubleshooting
Symptom	Likely Cause	Fix
No PWM output	MOE bit not set	Ensure TIM16->BDTR = 0x8000
Encoder not counting	Wrong alternate function	Verify PA0 and PB3 are AF1
Buzzer silent	TIM6 interrupt disabled	Check NVIC->ISER[1] bit 22
LED always off	Constant overspeed	Verify speed_rpm calculation
Display blank	SPI initialization failed	Check display power and connections
                   
Files in Project
File	Description
                   
main.c	Main program and peripheral initialization
display.h	SPI display driver
eeng1030_lib.h	Course library utilities
Makefile	Build configuration
Key Functions Reference
Function	Purpose
setup()	System-wide hardware initialization
initEncoder()	Configures TIM2 for quadrature encoder
initTimer16()	Sets up PWM output on PA6
initBuzzerTimer()	Configures TIM6 for buzzer tone
pinMode()	Sets GPIO direction
enablePullUp()	Enables internal pull-up resistor
delay_ms()	Millisecond blocking delay
                   
Safety Notes
The buzzer produces a 500 Hz tone at full volume during overspeed – protect hearing during testing
Ensure motor driver can handle the 25 kHz PWM switching frequency
The LED indicates safe operation (ON below 3500 RPM) – if LED remains off during normal speed, check encoder readings
